<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">26 May in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 26 May, from 451 to 1969.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-avarayr" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-avarayr.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/451">451 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,575 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Sasanian army defeats Armenian rebels at Avarayr</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">On the Avarayr plain, the army of the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-ii">Yazdegerd II</a>, who sought to impose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, defeated Armenian rebels led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardan-mamikonian">Vardan Mamikonian</a>, who was killed. The victory proved pyrrhic, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> were allowed to keep practising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> freely.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-avarayr">Battle of Avarayr</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/866">866 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,160 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Michael III crowns Basil I as Byzantine co-emperor</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Emperor Michael III crowned his favourite Basil, born to a peasant family, Byzantine co-emperor. Fearing a loss of influence, Basil had Michael assassinated the next year, becoming sole ruler and founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/946">946 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,080 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Eadred becomes king of the English after Edmund I is killed</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After King Edmund I was killed in a brawl with an outlaw at Pucklechurch, his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eadred">Eadred</a> became king, as Edmund's sons Eadwig and Edgar were young children. Eadred's reign was dominated by the struggle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eadred">Eadred</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/961">961 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,065 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Otto I has his son Otto II crowned at Aachen</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a>, who had named his six-year-old son Otto II heir apparent and co-ruler at Worms, had him crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen-cathedral">Aachen Cathedral</a>. Otto was preparing to march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for his imperial coronation.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Otto II</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1637">1637 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">389 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">English colonists destroy the Pequot fort at Mystic</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> forces and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohegan">Mohegan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narragansett-people">Narragansett</a> allies set fire to the fortified village, killing some 400 to 700 Pequot, many of them women and children. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pequot-war">Pequot War</a> ended months later.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1868">1868 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">158 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Last public hanging takes place in England</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Michael Barrett, convicted of the Clerkenwell bombing, was hanged before a crowd outside Newgate Prison. Three days later a new Act moved executions inside prison walls.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1896">1896 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">130 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Nicholas II is crowned Tsar of Russia</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> and Alexandra were crowned in the Kremlin's Dormition Cathedral. Days later more than a thousand people died in a crush at the celebrations on Khodynka Field, a grim omen for the reign.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1940">1940 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">86 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Evacuation of Allied troops from Dunkirk begins</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Operation Dynamo began. By 4 June more than 338,000 British and French soldiers had been carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> by warships and hundreds of civilian 'little ships'.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1969">1969 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">57 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Apollo 10 splashes down after the Moon landing rehearsal</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> 10 returned safely after its lunar module descended to about 15 km above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhu">Moon</a>. The mission tested every step of a landing except the touchdown itself.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 26 May <span class="n">5</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pope-clement-vii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Pope Clement VII</span><span class="tp">1478–1534</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">John Churchill, 1st Duke of Marlborough</span><span class="tp">1650–1722</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/nikolaus-ludwig-von-zinzendorf"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/nikolaus-ludwig-von-zinzendorf.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Nikolaus Ludwig von Zinzendorf</span><span class="tp">1700–1760</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/janos-kadar"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">János Kádár</span><span class="tp">1912–1989</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/frederik-x"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederik-x.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Frederik X</span><span class="tp">b. 1968</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 26 May <span class="n">4</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bayezid-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Bayezid II</span><span class="tp">1447–1512</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-elector-palatine"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/charles-ii-elector-palatine.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Charles II, Elector Palatine</span><span class="tp">1651–1685</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/samuel-pepys"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/samuel-pepys.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Samuel Pepys</span><span class="tp">1633–1703</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/emir-abdelkader"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/emir-abdelkader.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Emir Abdelkader</span><span class="tp">1808–1883</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
